= BITwiki interdisciplinary and informational scope =

'''BITwiki is an interdisciplinary general encyclopedia and a computation- and information-centered knowledge system.''' Information and computation are organizing perspectives and major domains of inquiry, '''not exclusionary admission requirements'''. Mathematics, physics, chemistry, biology, ecology, medicine, engineering, philosophy, language, history, culture, and other durable subjects can have their own canonical articles without a demonstration that the subject is literally a computer.

BITwiki's distinctive contribution is '''how it represents, sources, connects, retrieves, and evolves knowledge''' through human-readable articles, a semantic graph and programmable interfaces. Scientific disciplines, phenomena, systems, methods, and interpretations can coexist as independently meaningful objects. Computational models of those subjects may merit separate coverage, but do not displace the subjects they study.

This guidance applies the [[BITwiki:Constitution|Constitution]] as BITwiki's sole founding instrument. The superseded Charter remains preserved in Git history as source lineage, not an alternative editorial authority.

== Canonical inclusion and editorial purpose (K01) ==

K01 is a '''human editorial review of knowledge value and article quality''', not a computation-only gate. For a new or substantially revised Core subject, ask:

# '''Identity.''' Is the title accurate, appropriately scoped and distinguishable from existing pages?
# '''Substance.''' Does the article provide independently useful, coherent and durable knowledge without empty scaffolding or redundant fragments?
# '''Evidence.''' Are consequential claims grounded in appropriate sources? Are uncertainty, competing interpretations, hypotheses, models and limits labeled accurately?
# '''Integration.''' Are actual relationships to other disciplines and, when useful, informational or computational connections explained without invented analogies or unjustified semantic edges?
# '''Preservation.''' Are provenance, reader depths, links, related identities and revision history maintained during meaningful edits or migrations?

'''No separate computational-method or information-processing mechanism is necessary to justify a general scientific, historical or philosophical article.''' A topic's relevance may be disciplinary, foundational, practical, conceptual, historical or interdisciplinary. Asking why a page exists is different from demanding that it describe an algorithm.

Static tooling may mark K01 <code>UNVERIFIED</code> because it cannot determine scientific validity or editorial merit. This status is '''not''' a rejection of a subject outside computer science. Passing CI is technical validation, not an editorial or scientific certificate.

== Information, computation, models and speculative interpretations ==

BITwiki may study information-oriented cosmologies, the holographic principle, digital physics, the simulation hypothesis, and philosophical or physical interpretations of the universe. Such material can be included '''with explicit epistemic classification'''. The holographic principle and the simulation hypothesis are distinct; neither establishes that the universe is a literally programmed computer simulation.

Distinguish (1) empirically supported information-processing mechanisms; (2) computational models built by researchers to represent a system; and (3) philosophical metaphors, hypotheses and worldviews. These categories may all be encyclopedically valuable, but their claims must not be conflated.

Information has different formal meanings across information theory, physics, biology, language and philosophy. An interdisciplinary connection should identify which meaning is being used when that distinction matters.

